Sorry to reply to a very old thread, but this idea started to tingle in
my mind. I think it's an absolutely marvelous idea. When you think
about it, the concept of having a single character represent the whole
entity is a common phenomenon in both roguelikes and in chinese. We've
all propably had confusing experiences when a "D" in some other context
(not nethack) wakes the first association of a dragon, rather than just
a capital d. The idea of translating the game to a language that has
this feature built-in is a fantastic idea. If you could replace every
monster & item symbol with characters that actually mean what they
represent, it would propably be an easier game for a newbie to start.
But then, that would be much more than just a translation, propably a
whole new version (Cnethack?).

This whole idea makes me wonder, why roguelikes were not originally
created in China or Japan. Perhaps more propably Japan, since I would
think they had more computers in the 1980's.

But then a few a bit off-topic questions to Joe Doufu or anyone who
might know:
